  Two things I found that you should know about. I'm not acting on either yet:
  - [1mThe table needs JavaScript to show anything.[0m [38;5;153mactivity.js[39m builds every row, so with JS off the table is empty. That
    breaks the NOTES.md rule that content must work without JS.
  - [1mNo token stylesheet is loaded.[0m Nothing in [38;5;153mpublic/[39m loads the shared token sheet, and the guidelines require every new
    style to use it. I'll need to find out where it comes from before I write any CSS.

  [1mFirst question: what should people be able to narrow the table by?[0m Each answer changes how big the diff gets and which
  controls the guidelines require.

  - [1mEvent type only, plus a "Failed sign-ins" shortcut.[0m This goes straight at the only support tickets so far. It's one
    [38;5;153mSelect[39m (nine options) and a button, so the diff is small. It doesn't help someone looking for "that thing from last
    Tuesday", but other filters can be added later without redoing this one.
  - [1mEvent type plus date range.[0m This also covers "when did this happen." Per the forms guide it adds two date pickers, a
    presets [38;5;153mSelect[39m and date-order validation, which roughly doubles the controls and the review surface.
  - [1mEvent type plus a text search over device, location and details.[0m This catches "anything from Frankfurt" or "the
    Firefox one." Search has its own rules: clear button, Escape to clear, no validation on every keystroke. It also
    matches against masked IPs, which is harmless but can be confusing.
  - [1mAll of the above.[0m This is the most capable option, but it will likely go past the ~300-line limit and need splitting
    into more than one commit or PR.

  I recommend the first: event type plus the failed sign-ins shortcut. It solves the problem we actually have evidence
  for, keeps the diff small, and leaves room to add the others later.
